{"name":"Pony Tail","hex":"#d2bc9b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pony tail","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of ponytail."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pony_tail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ponytail","blocks":[{"text":"A ponytail is a hairstyle in which some, most, or all of the hair on the head is pulled away from the face, gathered, and secured at the back of the head with a hair tie, clip, or other similar accessory and allowed to hang freely from that point. The style gets its name from its resemblance to the tail of a pony."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Avatar_The_Way_of_Water_Tokyo_Press_Conference_Zoe_Salda%C3%B1a_%2852563505738%29_%28cropped%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Avatar_The_Way_of_Water_Tokyo_Press_Conference_Zoe_Salda%C3%B1a_%2852563505738%29_%28cropped%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponytail"},{"title":"Pony","blocks":[{"text":"A pony is a type of small horse, usually under a specific height at full maturity. Ponies often have thicker coats, manes and tails, compared to larger horses, and proportionally shorter legs, wider barrels, heavier bone, thicker necks and shorter heads. In modern use, breed registries and horse shows may define a pony as measuring at the withers below a certain height; height limits varying from about 14 hands to 14.3 hands. Some distinguish between horse or pony based on its breed or phenotype, regardless of its height. The word pony derives from the old French poulenet, a diminutive of poulain meaning foal, a young, immature horse. A full-sized horse may sometimes be called a pony as a term of endearment."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Gracie-rhs2005.jpg/330px-Gracie-rhs2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pony"},{"title":"Tail","blocks":[{"text":"The tail is the elongated section at the rear end of a bilaterian animal's body; in general, the term refers to a distinct, flexible appendage extending backwards from the midline of the torso. In vertebrate animals that evolved to lose their tails, the coccyx is the homologous vestigial of the tail. While tails are primarily considered a feature of vertebrates, some invertebrates such as scorpions and springtails, as well as snails and slugs, have tail-like appendages that are also referred to as tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/White-tailed_deer%2C_tail_up.jpg/330px-White-tailed_deer%2C_tail_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tail"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Tied_Ponytail.JPG/500px-Tied_Ponytail.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Tied_Ponytail.JPG/1280px-Tied_Ponytail.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Tied_Ponytail.JPG/500px-Tied_Ponytail.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A tied ponytail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATied_Ponytail.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Girl_with_ponytail-Women_at_the_Quern.jpg/500px-Girl_with_ponytail-Women_at_the_Quern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Girl_with_ponytail-Women_at_the_Quern.jpg/960px-Girl_with_ponytail-Women_at_the_Quern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Girl_with_ponytail-Women_at_the_Quern.jpg